message.content:
{
  "summary": "This bill would limit the liability of higher education institutions in negligence claims involving people with prior criminal convictions. Under the proposal, a higher education institution would not be held liable for negligence in admitting, enrolling, providing housing, or otherwise facilitating the education of a person solely because that person had a prior criminal conviction. Additionally, evidence of such a conviction would be inadmissible in these types of lawsuits. These protections would not apply if the institution had actual knowledge that the person was convicted of a violent offense or a violent sexual offense, or if the institution willfully allowed a person to continue their education or residency after having actual knowledge of such an offense. This bill does not create or expand any causes of action. The definition of a higher education institution includes schools, colleges, universities, or other entities providing credentials or instruction to people who have finished secondary education or are past the age of compulsory high school attendance. If passed, this act would take effect July 1, 2026, and apply to claims arising on or after that date."
}
